  \]
- For \(p = 11\):
  \[
  m = 11^3 = 1331 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Sum} = 1 + 11 + 121 + 1331 = 1464
  \]
- For \(p = 13\):
  \[
  m = 13^3 = 2197 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Sum} = 1 + 13 + 169 + 2197 = 2380
  \]

None of these sums fall within our range of interest.

#### Case 2: \(m = pq\)
Next, we will check combinations of distinct primes \(p\) and \(q\):
- For \(p = 2\) and \(q = 3\):
  \[
  m = 2 \cdot 3 = 6 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Sum} = (2 + 1)(3 + 1) = 3 \cdot 4 = 12
  \]
- For \(p = 2\) and \(q = 5\):
  \[
  m = 2 \cdot 5 = 10 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Sum} = (2 + 1)(5 + 1) = 3 \cdot 6 = 18
  \]
- For \(p = 2\) and \(q = 7\):
  \[
  m = 2 \cdot 7 = 14 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Sum} = (2 + 1)(7 + 1) = 3 \cdot 8 = 24
  \]
- For \(p = 3\) and \(q = 5\):
  \[
  m = 3 \cdot 5 = 15 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Sum} = (3 + 1)(5 + 1) = 4 \cdot 6 = 24
  \]
- For \(p = 3\) and \(q = 7\):
  \[
  m = 3 \cdot 7 = 21 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Sum} = (3 + 1)(7 + 1) = 4 \cdot 8 = 32
  \]
- For \(p = 5\) and \(q = 7\):
  \[
  m = 5 \cdot 7 = 35 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Sum} = (5 + 1)(7 + 1) = 6 \cdot 8 = 48
  \]

Continuing this process, we find the following sums that fall within our range:
- \(m = 2 \cdot 11 = 22\) gives \(3 \cdot 12 = 36\)
- \(m = 3 \cdot 11 = 33\) gives \(4 \cdot 12 = 48\)
- \(m = 2 \cdot 13 = 26\) gives \(3 \cdot 14 = 42\)
- \(m = 3 \cdot 13 = 39\) gives \(4 \cdot 14 = 56\)
- \(m = 5 \cdot 11 = 55\) gives \(6 \cdot 12 = 72\)

After checking all combinations, we find that the nice numbers in the range \(\{2010, 2011, 2012, \dots, 2019\}\) are:
